WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.000
Keep the same carriers and per-site rates. An occupied site cannot bind a second nutrient at the same time.

00:12.000 --> 00:22.000
Higher maintained concentration increases binding frequency and reduces waiting. The per-site rate constants remain unchanged.

00:22.000 --> 00:32.000
Gold marks occupied sites; a green pulse marks transport. Some bound nitrate returns outside. Individual sites fluctuate; the curve describes a steady population average.

00:32.000 --> 00:50.000
In this two-state model, uptake equals capacity times the occupied fraction. Increasing concentration raises occupancy, giving a saturating curve.

00:50.000 --> 01:04.000
At surface concentration S equal to Ks, uptake is half of Vmax. Vmax is approached asymptotically, rather than reached at a finite concentration.

01:04.000 --> 01:22.000
Doubling S from 1 to 2 increases uptake by 71.4 percent. Doubling S from 20 to 40 increases uptake by 11.1 percent. Vmax and Ks remain fixed.

01:22.000 --> 01:40.000
Double the number of identical carriers per gram. In this model Vmax doubles, while Ks is unchanged. Uptake doubles at every maintained concentration.

01:40.000 --> 01:56.000
At equal Vmax, lower Ks gives greater uptake at the same concentration. Ks is a half-saturation concentration, not generally an equilibrium binding constant.

01:56.000 --> 02:04.000
Which population takes up more at S equal to 0.5? A has Vmax 4 and Ks 1. B has Vmax 12 and Ks 2. Predict before calculating.

02:04.000 --> 02:20.000
The initial slope alpha equals Vmax divided by Ks. B has the higher Ks but also the steeper initial slope: 6 rather than 4. At S equal to 0.5, B takes up 2.40 and A 1.33, in the same uptake units.

02:20.000 --> 02:28.000
Next, let nitrate concentration fall in a closed flask. Measure the depletion curve, account for volume and biomass, and relate uptake rate to concentration.
